class MyQueue {

    Stack<Integer> input = new Stack<>();
    Stack<Integer> output = new Stack<>();

    public void push(int x) {
        input.push(x);
    }

    public int pop() {
        peek();
        return output.pop();
    }

    public int peek() {
        if (output.empty()) {
            while (!input.empty()) {
                output.push(input.pop());
            }
        }
        return output.peek();
    }

    public boolean empty() {
        return input.empty() && output.empty();
    }
}


// I’m implementing a Queue using two Stacks.

// I use one stack called input to store the elements when I push them.

// For pop() and peek(), I use the output stack. If the output stack is empty, I move all elements from input to output. This reverses their order, so the element that was inserted first comes to the top.

// For example, if I push 1, 2, and 3, the input stack contains 1, 2, 3. When I transfer them to the output stack, the order becomes 3, 2, 1, so 1 is on top and can be removed first.

// This gives me FIFO behavior, which is the basic property of a Queue.

// The push() operation takes O(1), while pop() and peek() are amortized O(1), because each element is transferred from one stack to the other at most once.”
